            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+geometric+Langlands+correspondence"} quantum geometric Langlands correspondence
            that identifies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+D-modules"} twisted D-modules
            on both sides.
          %p
            For the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian
            case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            the above equivalence has indeed been proven, given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier-Mukai+transform"} Fourier-Mukai transform
            (
            %a{:href => "#Laumon85"}> Laumon 85
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Laumon96"} Laumon 96
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Rothstein96"} Rothstein 96
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#AbelianCase"} below
          %p#However
            However, in general the above version of the conjecture is false. For instance it fails in the case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %msub
